\section{Which quartile definition?}{

Quartiles have no single definition. The assessment example,
\code{c(1, 2, 2, 3, 4, 5, 5, 5, 6, 10)}, expects \code{Q1 = 2.5} and \code{Q3 = 5.5}, and
none of the nine types of \code{\link[stats:quantile]{stats::quantile()}} reproduces that: type 7 (the R
default) gives 2.25 and 5, type 6 gives 2 and 5.25, and the median of each
half gives 2 and 5. The simple rule that does is the default
\code{type = "assessment"}: after sorting, take position \code{h = p * n + 1} (capped
at \code{n}) and interpolate linearly between the two neighbouring values.
Use \code{type = 1:9} to get the matching \code{\link[stats:quantile]{stats::quantile()}} type instead, for
example \code{type = 7} to agree with \code{quantile(x, 0.25)}.
}

